WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.800
Nickelodeon's Doug is still a major source
of nostalgia for early millennials who grew

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.080
up in the splat-TV era. The diary-style cartoon
series centered on the life of a boy in small-town

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.579
Bluffington as he grappled with the typical
issues of youth — from navigating an unrequited

00:00:14.579 --> 00:00:19.490
infatuation to concealing a superhero alter
ego. Doug Funnie's life was both relatable

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:23.580
and charming to watch. But what you may not
know about the series is how it became so

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:27.760
beloved in the first place — and how it
hit a few major speed bumps along the way.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.140
Here's a look back at some things you might
not know about Doug.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.260
The life of Brian

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.579
It’s hard to imagine Doug as being about
anyone except, well, Doug -- but creator Jim

00:00:38.579 --> 00:00:43.519
Jinkins originally had another name in mind.
In 2012, Jinkins told Splitsider that since

00:00:43.519 --> 00:00:47.480
the character was supposed to have a very
"any guy" look, he wanted to name him something

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.219
completely blasé. Jinkins explained,

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:54.559
"At the beginning, I was calling him 'Brian.'
It was sort of just a generic name. [...] I

00:00:54.559 --> 00:00:58.530
thought that 'Doug' had that feeling and that
sound about it. It was a conscious choice

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:02.020
because I wanted him to be this very neutral,
middle-of-the-road guy."

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:06.520
Doug's middle name, Yancey, also had some
significance for Jinkins, as it was shared

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:11.320
by own his grandfather. Doug's embarrassment
ov er it stemmed from a very real place, as

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:16.460
he had a childhood friend who also hated his
own name growing up, much to Jinkins' amusement.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:19.100
“Hello there, Yancey!”

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:22.200
“My name’s Doug."

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:23.640
Ad man

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:28.700
Jinkins' Doug doodles originally existed as
a sort of "alter ego" for the artist, who

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:32.399
said he desperately needed an outlet from
the real world, where problems are a lot more

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.220
complicated than they are for the residents
of his pseudo-utopia Bluffington. Jinkins

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.720
once explained of the character's origin,

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.400
"I like to doodle, [and] Doug was just one
of those guys I kept doodling over and over.”

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:47.820
Commercial watchers might even remember a
very early version of Doug bouncing around

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:52.750
on a pogo stick in his signature vest for
a 1989 Florida grapefruit juice commercial,

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:57.980
and he and his prized pooch Porkchop were
later seen in a 1990 USA Network commercial.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.060
Bluffington is real

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.420
Eventually, Jinkins collected his drawings
into a book called Doug Got a New Pair of

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:09.539
Shoes and sent a copy to a Nickelodeon executive,
which led to the network ordering an eight-minute

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:13.879
pilot episode called "Doug Can't Dance." Jinkins
immediately went to work creating his own

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:15.460
production studio to make it happen.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:20.000
As he began developing other characters for
the series, Jinkins again drew inspiration

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.680
from his own life and the people he knew.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.580
“He’s kind of how I remember childhood,
growing up…”

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:29.620
Doug's love interest Patti Mayonnaise, for
example, was based on Jinkins' own childhood

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:34.251
crush. Supporting characters Roger Klotz and
Doug’s best friend, Skeeter Valentine, were

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.320
also based on people Jinkins grew up with.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:38.560
No instruments needed

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:44.000
You probably know that mega-famous voice actor
Billy West, also known for voicing the title

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:48.000
characters for Nickelodeon’s popular cartoon
series The Ren &amp; Stimpy Show, was cast to

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:48.720
portray Doug.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:51.460
“...he’s a painfully shy 11 ½ year old.”

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:55.820
However, West wasn't the only voice actor
to make a major impact on the show. Actor

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:59.970
Fred Newman was brought on to voice some of
the zanier-sounding characters — including

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:01.620
Porkchop, Skeeter, and Mr. Dink.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:07.860
“You can call me Mr. Dink.”

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:12.700
Newman even ended up creating that earworm
of a theme song using the simplest of tools.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:14.840
Of that catchy intro, Newman explained,

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.150
"That wasn't Doug's voice, that was actually
my voice. [...] We wanted to use as much as

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:23.650
we could household objects, things that a
kid could have, make it sound innocent.”

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:28.189
That’s right -- Newman relied on his unique
beatboxing and "honking" skills to create

00:03:28.189 --> 00:03:30.620
a lot of the sounds heard throughout the catchy
intro.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:37.840
The rainbow rebuke

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.879
The title character of Doug and his family
were drawn to be very obviously Caucasian,

00:03:42.879 --> 00:03:47.560
but many other characters in the series boasted
rather unusual skin colors — from Skeeter's

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:53.710
teal complexion, to Roger's lime green tone,
to Beebe Bluff's purplish appearance. However,

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:57.290
Jinkins insists he wasn't trying to make any
major socio-political statements with his

00:03:57.290 --> 00:03:58.700
colorful cast of characters.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:02.900
Jinkins told HuffPost of the controversy over
his main character's skin tone:

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:07.890
"People are certain that Skeeter is an African-American
guy. I'm like, ‘Well, he's blue!' [...] If

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:12.140
that's how you see him, that's great. It's
not a bad thing, but I never planned it. Skeeter

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:13.540
is blue and he's Doug's friend."

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:15.600
A network tangle

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.180
Following its original 52-episode run from
1991 to 1994, Nickelodeon declined an option

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:27.560
to renew Doug for a new season, despite Jinkins
being signed for 65 episodes. The decision,

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:32.740
which reportedly came as a shock, was based
upon some budget tightening underway at Nickelodeon.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:36.810
However, Nickelodeon’s voluntary loss was
Disney's gain, as it allowed the House of

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:40.750
Mouse to scoop up the production rights and
studio for Doug and proceed with carrying

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:45.590
the hit show forward. The show was retooled
as Brand Spanking New! Doug as part of the

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:50.470
ABC Saturday Morning Cartoon lineup for three
additional seasons and spawned a live-action

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:55.430
musical, a line of toy merchandise with fast-food
cross-marketing, and even a film called Doug's

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:55.960
First Movie.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.480
A not so happy ending

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:03.580
The saga of Doug Funnie and Patti Mayonnaise
never quite came to a close, leaving fans

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:07.300
to wonder whether the green-vested gentleman
managed to win Patti’s heart for the long

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:11.930
haul. According to Jinkins, the answer is
likely no. He told Entertainment Weekly that

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:15.600
he couldn't picture the two ending up together
as adults because it's just not realistic,

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:16.100
saying,

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.260
"Most people don't end up with their first
love."

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:22.560
Perhaps Jinkins is merely jaded, based on
his own experience later in life with the

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.810
real-life person who inspired Patti. In a
Nic k Animation podcast, he recounted his

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:31.380
reunion with the woman in question and admitted
that they didn't enjoy some sudden romance,

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:35.871
despite his hopes that it might happen. After
she reached out to invite him to dinner, Jinkins

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:39.590
admitted to wondering if something might spark…
until she said,

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.920
"Oh, I want you to meet my husband!"

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:43.100
Sorry, Doug.

